WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:02.773
(upbeat music)

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:06.080
- Good morning, everyone,

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:07.660
perhaps good afternoon, good evening

00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:09.330
wherever you're joining us from.

00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:11.520
It's really a pleasure to be here,

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:14.730
I always enjoy spending
time with participants

00:00:14.730 --> 00:00:17.620
in the CIFAR machine
learning, deep learning,

00:00:17.620 --> 00:00:19.830
reinforcement learning summer school.

00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:22.280
I'm a little bit sad we
can't do it in person

00:00:22.280 --> 00:00:25.330
but really happy we have
time together today.

00:00:25.330 --> 00:00:29.880
I'll share with you some of
the work that we've been doing.

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:33.490
This is work that's primarily
done with some of my students

00:00:33.490 --> 00:00:36.030
at McGill University
over the last few years,

00:00:36.030 --> 00:00:39.190
but I will say since
then it has really grown

00:00:39.190 --> 00:00:40.500
throughout the community

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:43.810
and I've had the pleasure
of working on this topic

00:00:43.810 --> 00:00:47.460
of how do we set up a
better set of experiments

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:49.430
for research and reinforcement learning

00:00:49.430 --> 00:00:53.060
with people across the planet
who are really deeply involved

00:00:53.060 --> 00:00:56.290
in raising our standards
in terms of the quality

00:00:56.290 --> 00:00:58.030
of our experimental work.

00:00:58.030 --> 00:01:00.570
And at the end of the talk,
hopefully I'll get there,

00:01:00.570 --> 00:01:02.710
I will even have some suggestions and tips

00:01:02.710 --> 00:01:05.510
if any of you want to get
involved into this work,

00:01:05.510 --> 00:01:08.520
there's lots of ways that
you can actually participate

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:09.633
towards this effort.

00:01:10.950 --> 00:01:12.093
Getting started,

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:16.670
one of the main reasons
I'm really interested

00:01:16.670 --> 00:01:19.360
in making sure that we
have good experiments,

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:21.820
and we'll spend some time
sort of discussing today

00:01:21.820 --> 00:01:23.960
what are the conditions for doing that

00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:28.410
is because we are seeing the
speed at which AI in general,

00:01:28.410 --> 00:01:31.210
but reinforcement learning
more specifically,

00:01:31.210 --> 00:01:34.640
that we're moving from research
to technology development,

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:36.630
to deployed applications.

00:01:36.630 --> 00:01:39.240
So really this pipeline of innovation

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:41.390
from really the inception of an idea

00:01:41.390 --> 00:01:44.550
to having a product working in the world

00:01:44.550 --> 00:01:47.670
is getting shorter over time,

00:01:47.670 --> 00:01:51.380
whereas in other fields that
we've seen much, much longer

00:01:51.380 --> 00:01:52.750
development time in AI

00:01:52.750 --> 00:01:55.070
often it's the case that
a new algorithm comes up

00:01:55.070 --> 00:01:56.220
and, you know, three months later

00:01:56.220 --> 00:01:58.330
it's actually running and brought up.

00:01:58.330 --> 00:02:01.940
And so that means that we have
really a high responsibility

00:02:01.940 --> 00:02:04.260
in terms of making sure
that each of the step

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:06.600
of the development that we invest in

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:08.570
is actually done in a way

00:02:08.570 --> 00:02:11.190
that meets our high standards of quality,

00:02:11.190 --> 00:02:13.270
whether we're building navigation systems

00:02:13.270 --> 00:02:14.640
for autonomous vehicles,

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:18.430
whether we're building chatbot
agents to allow interactions

00:02:18.430 --> 00:02:20.190
between human and machines,

00:02:20.190 --> 00:02:24.580
or whether we're developing and
deploying intelligent robots

00:02:24.580 --> 00:02:25.900
to assist in surgery,

00:02:25.900 --> 00:02:28.350
all of these cases where people have used

00:02:28.350 --> 00:02:31.090
reinforcement learning
to improve the behavior

00:02:31.090 --> 00:02:32.650
of the system,

00:02:32.650 --> 00:02:35.140
I assure you that no matter
which of these system,

00:02:35.140 --> 00:02:38.900
I'm also including top left an
example of energy management,

00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:40.920
smart cities and grid system,

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:44.930
where we also have seen really
promising RL applications.

00:02:44.930 --> 00:02:46.550
In all of these cases,

00:02:46.550 --> 00:02:48.980
we want to make sure that when we run

00:02:48.980 --> 00:02:52.430
a reinforcement learning
agent in real time,

00:02:52.430 --> 00:02:54.860
that the performance
is going to be adequate

00:02:54.860 --> 00:02:58.400
based on our expectation that
it's not going to cause harm,

00:02:58.400 --> 00:02:59.960
that we can predict in advance

00:02:59.960 --> 00:03:03.560
what will be the quality
level of that system.

00:03:03.560 --> 00:03:05.440
And so that's really my deep motivation

00:03:05.440 --> 00:03:07.660
and I'll share with you some applications

00:03:07.660 --> 00:03:08.930
that we've worked in in our lab

00:03:08.930 --> 00:03:12.400
that have motivated that more
immediately in our own work.

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:14.130
But really thinking broadly

00:03:14.130 --> 00:03:16.380
about why do I think it's important for us

00:03:16.380 --> 00:03:19.810
today together to speak about
conducting good experiments

00:03:19.810 --> 00:03:21.973
this is the place where I'm coming from.

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:27.570
Now assume by now you
have a pretty good sense

00:03:27.570 --> 00:03:29.800
of how to build a machine
learning pipeline,

00:03:29.800 --> 00:03:31.880
and this is a very generic one,

00:03:31.880 --> 00:03:33.880
but in many case you have this first phase

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:35.210
of getting the data,

00:03:35.210 --> 00:03:37.380
and then there's another
phase of cleaning, preparing,

00:03:37.380 --> 00:03:39.030
manipulating the data,

00:03:39.030 --> 00:03:42.320
you may be training your
model, test your model,

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:43.770
after that and then you improve

00:03:43.770 --> 00:03:47.340
and see what phase of the
system you need to go back to.

00:03:47.340 --> 00:03:51.200
This is a pretty standard pipeline.

00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:53.100
In reality, in each of these steps

00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:55.940
there's a lot of decisions
that have to be made

00:03:55.940 --> 00:03:58.800
about how you are going to set up this,

00:03:58.800 --> 00:03:59.810
and this is what I'm calling

00:03:59.810 --> 00:04:01.290
sort of the experimental pipeline,

00:04:01.290 --> 00:04:04.750
how you're going to set
up each of these steps.

00:04:04.750 --> 00:04:08.400
And if you're not careful
about really being precise

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:10.130
about your design choices

00:04:10.130 --> 00:04:14.620
and furthermore documenting
them in a transparent way,

00:04:14.620 --> 00:04:19.050
and can be quite difficult to
verify the quality of the work

00:04:19.050 --> 00:04:20.560
that you're doing.

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:24.120
And so in a lot of the work that I do,

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:28.000
one of the key concepts that
arises in this discussion

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:32.730
of better experiments is the
notion of reproducibility.

00:04:32.730 --> 00:04:35.450
And so reproducibility is really all about

00:04:35.450 --> 00:04:38.850
having the ability to
duplicate an experiment,

00:04:38.850 --> 00:04:41.320
to duplicate the results.

00:04:41.320 --> 00:04:43.610
The goal of reproducibility

00:04:43.610 --> 00:04:47.100
is really that we want
to achieve robustness,

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:51.540
and so the idea is that
if you run an experiment

00:04:51.540 --> 00:04:52.610
and if you're able to run,

00:04:52.610 --> 00:04:54.220
you or someone else is able to run

00:04:54.220 --> 00:04:56.470
that same experiment again,

00:04:56.470 --> 00:04:59.620
then you can have higher
confidence that the findings

00:04:59.620 --> 00:05:01.480
or the conclusions of that experiments

00:05:01.480 --> 00:05:04.060
are actually of a high-quality.

00:05:04.060 --> 00:05:06.270
And this concept of reproducibility

00:05:06.270 --> 00:05:08.470
actually permeates the world of science

00:05:08.470 --> 00:05:11.600
it's not specific to AI
or reinforcement learning,

00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:15.183
that's really very fundamental
to the scientific method.

00:05:16.290 --> 00:05:19.920
And one of the reasons we
really emphasize reproducibility

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:23.670
is because it gives us this
high confidence in our findings.

00:05:23.670 --> 00:05:26.510
And so really it will be the major theme

00:05:26.510 --> 00:05:27.860
of what I talk about today,

00:05:27.860 --> 00:05:29.870
when I talk about better experiments,

00:05:29.870 --> 00:05:31.460
what really we're talking about

00:05:31.460 --> 00:05:35.110
is how do we build experiments
that are reproducible,

00:05:35.110 --> 00:05:37.390
experiments where we
can have high confidence

00:05:37.390 --> 00:05:39.310
in the conclusions that we're drawing

00:05:39.310 --> 00:05:42.033
from the results that are obtained.

00:05:44.940 --> 00:05:46.760
There's actually been a lot of discussion

00:05:46.760 --> 00:05:50.040
on the last few years
about what is the state

00:05:50.040 --> 00:05:52.670
of reproducibility in science in general?

00:05:52.670 --> 00:05:55.420
So I'm sharing here are the
quick snapshot of an article

00:05:55.420 --> 00:05:58.640
published in the journal nature of 2016.

00:05:58.640 --> 00:06:01.560
We are 1500 or so researchers were asked

00:06:01.560 --> 00:06:05.210
whether they thought there
was a reproducibility crisis

00:06:05.210 --> 00:06:07.500
within their field of science,

00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:11.620
but 52% said, yes there's
a significant crisis,

00:06:11.620 --> 00:06:14.770
38% said maybe a slight crisis

00:06:14.770 --> 00:06:17.530
and a few of them didn't
seem to be very concerned

00:06:17.530 --> 00:06:18.997
about the situation.

00:06:20.460 --> 00:06:23.077
Now, beyond that, the
survey also asked, you know,

00:06:23.077 --> 00:06:26.590
"Have you ever failed to
reproduce an experiment?"

00:06:26.590 --> 00:06:28.530
And they asked both about whether you fail

00:06:28.530 --> 00:06:31.510
to reproduce someone else's
experiment, so in darker red,

00:06:31.510 --> 00:06:33.520
or whether you would fail to reproduce

00:06:33.520 --> 00:06:36.993
one of your own
experiments, in lighter red.

00:06:38.090 --> 00:06:40.670
And so what we see is that in many fields,

00:06:40.670 --> 00:06:42.720
chemistry, biology,

00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:46.400
upwards of 60% of researchers
have been the situation

00:06:46.400 --> 00:06:48.510
where they were unable to reproduce

00:06:48.510 --> 00:06:51.133
someone else's experiment
or their own experiment.

00:06:52.050 --> 00:06:54.370
The computer science
doesn't really detail there,

00:06:54.370 --> 00:06:56.750
we'll dig more into it today,

00:06:56.750 --> 00:06:59.300
but even in the physics and engineering

00:06:59.300 --> 00:07:02.140
where you would think
that perhaps it's easier

00:07:02.140 --> 00:07:05.090
to reproduce experiments
compared to dealing with

00:07:05.090 --> 00:07:07.620
maybe biology or chemistry,

00:07:07.620 --> 00:07:10.390
still a very, very high rate of inability

00:07:10.390 --> 00:07:13.190
to reproduce certain experiments.

00:07:13.190 --> 00:07:15.923
So that sort of sets the
stage for where we're at.

00:07:16.930 --> 00:07:19.360
In many ways when dealing
with machine learning,

00:07:19.360 --> 00:07:22.470
there's a sense that we
should be able to do better,

00:07:22.470 --> 00:07:25.600
you know, we can control much
more of the data that we use,

00:07:25.600 --> 00:07:27.500
the algorithms that are running,

00:07:27.500 --> 00:07:30.860
the machines on which these
algorithms are running.

00:07:30.860 --> 00:07:32.330
And so in doing that,

00:07:32.330 --> 00:07:35.230
we should be able to meet
a much higher standard

00:07:35.230 --> 00:07:38.230
of reproducibility than
perhaps some of these fields

00:07:38.230 --> 00:07:40.960
where there's a lot more
extraneous variables

00:07:40.960 --> 00:07:44.053
that might affect the
result of the research.

00:07:45.830 --> 00:07:48.640
So for those of you who are
interested in digging in

00:07:48.640 --> 00:07:51.990
and seeing how we're doing
overall in machine learning,

00:07:51.990 --> 00:07:54.420
I would recommend this very nice paper

00:07:54.420 --> 00:07:56.870
published just a few years ago.

00:07:56.870 --> 00:08:01.870
This researcher analyzed 250
so papers from several years

00:08:02.970 --> 00:08:05.050
of machine learning research

00:08:05.050 --> 00:08:07.470
and found that over that set of papers,

00:08:07.470 --> 00:08:11.960
around 63% of them are
deemed reproducible.

00:08:11.960 --> 00:08:15.110
We also dug in a little
bit more and analyzed

00:08:15.110 --> 00:08:18.750
what were the factors that
affected reproducibility.

00:08:18.750 --> 00:08:20.570
So these are factors
that I kind of come back

00:08:20.570 --> 00:08:23.010
in our discussion today.

00:08:23.010 --> 00:08:25.760
One of them is the
availability of pseudo code,

00:08:25.760 --> 00:08:29.070
even better in some
cases, open source code.

00:08:29.070 --> 00:08:31.480
One of them is a clear specification

00:08:31.480 --> 00:08:33.080
of what are the hyper-parameters

00:08:33.080 --> 00:08:36.020
that were considered in the experiments.

00:08:36.020 --> 00:08:38.730
Another one is the
readability of the equations

00:08:38.730 --> 00:08:40.610
and the tables.

00:08:40.610 --> 00:08:41.443
In some cases,

00:08:41.443 --> 00:08:43.440
if you're not able to really understand

00:08:43.440 --> 00:08:44.273
whether the results

00:08:44.273 --> 00:08:47.260
it's hard to make sure that
you are actually achieving them

00:08:47.260 --> 00:08:49.810
when you run the reproducibility study.

00:08:49.810 --> 00:08:52.370
A fourth one was the compute needed,

00:08:52.370 --> 00:08:54.890
there's some work that is being produced

00:08:54.890 --> 00:08:56.390
especially very recently,

00:08:56.390 --> 00:08:59.100
that requires enormous
amounts of computation

00:08:59.100 --> 00:09:01.590
which is perhaps not accessible.

00:09:01.590 --> 00:09:03.670
Last one was the topic of the work,

00:09:03.670 --> 00:09:05.410
so that seemed to be a main driver,

00:09:05.410 --> 00:09:08.560
there is some subtopics
of machine learning

00:09:08.560 --> 00:09:11.500
which are harder to reproduce than others.

00:09:11.500 --> 00:09:13.390
The last point which I
didn't highlight here,

00:09:13.390 --> 00:09:17.200
but also comes through if you
look at some of the P values

00:09:17.200 --> 00:09:20.390
which indicate the significance
of a particular feature

00:09:20.390 --> 00:09:25.390
and reproducibility is the
conversation with the authors.

00:09:26.060 --> 00:09:29.290
So the ability to go back
and forth with the author

00:09:29.290 --> 00:09:31.800
to have more detail about their experiment

00:09:31.800 --> 00:09:34.130
turned out to be a determining factor

00:09:34.130 --> 00:09:37.010
in the ability to reproduce experiments.

00:09:37.010 --> 00:09:39.530
So quite interesting to keep in mind,

00:09:39.530 --> 00:09:42.370
and I encourage each of you is interested

00:09:42.370 --> 00:09:44.780
to go in and read the paper

00:09:44.780 --> 00:09:48.830
and really learn what we can
from these many, many years

00:09:48.830 --> 00:09:50.480
of experiments in machine learning

00:09:50.480 --> 00:09:52.930
and the very nice analysis that was done.

00:09:52.930 --> 00:09:56.370
That really starts giving
you a sense of what matters,

00:09:56.370 --> 00:10:00.600
what are the types of
procedures and good habits

00:10:00.600 --> 00:10:03.080
you need to build into
your own experiments

00:10:03.080 --> 00:10:05.580
as you've ventured into
machine learning research.

00:10:06.540 --> 00:10:09.540
Now, I want to just give a
little bit of a landscape

00:10:09.540 --> 00:10:11.720
in terms of different terms

00:10:11.720 --> 00:10:15.000
that are used across the community.

00:10:15.000 --> 00:10:17.220
I will say with a bit of a caveat

00:10:17.220 --> 00:10:20.320
that these terms are not
used always in the same way

00:10:20.320 --> 00:10:21.980
across these disciplines,

00:10:21.980 --> 00:10:23.610
I'm basing this by report

00:10:23.610 --> 00:10:26.340
that the NSF National Science
Foundation in the U.S.

00:10:26.340 --> 00:10:30.470
has issued recently about
the notion of reproducibility

00:10:30.470 --> 00:10:33.470
trying to crystallize the
definition of these terms,

00:10:33.470 --> 00:10:35.370
so that's the one I'm adopting today,

00:10:35.370 --> 00:10:36.500
but I will just warn you

00:10:36.500 --> 00:10:37.870
if you're digging into this literature

00:10:37.870 --> 00:10:40.100
that there has been some inconsistency

00:10:40.100 --> 00:10:42.410
in how that's used in the literature.

00:10:42.410 --> 00:10:44.180
For the purposes of today,

00:10:44.180 --> 00:10:47.830
I'm going to assume that
an experiment result

00:10:47.830 --> 00:10:51.560
is reproducible when we are able

00:10:51.560 --> 00:10:54.640
to obtain the same conclusion
using the same code

00:10:54.640 --> 00:10:58.030
in analysis and using the same data

00:10:58.030 --> 00:11:02.860
as the original experiment.

00:11:02.860 --> 00:11:05.700
And so that's really in a
sense that minimum standard,

00:11:05.700 --> 00:11:06.980
everything else on this board

00:11:06.980 --> 00:11:08.850
is harder than reproducibility,

00:11:08.850 --> 00:11:10.420
but yet that is where I'm starting

00:11:10.420 --> 00:11:12.570
because I still think we
have a lot of work to do

00:11:12.570 --> 00:11:13.553
to achieve this.

00:11:14.620 --> 00:11:16.310
The notion of replicability

00:11:16.310 --> 00:11:18.220
is when you actually apply the same code,

00:11:18.220 --> 00:11:20.960
the same analysis, but on
data that is different,

00:11:20.960 --> 00:11:23.620
so maybe you went ahead and
collected another data set,

00:11:23.620 --> 00:11:26.520
and you see whether that's
actually replicable.

00:11:26.520 --> 00:11:28.460
The notion of robustness

00:11:28.460 --> 00:11:31.000
is when you might use the same data,

00:11:31.000 --> 00:11:33.480
but you might have some
changes in terms of the code

00:11:33.480 --> 00:11:34.560
or the analysis,

00:11:34.560 --> 00:11:36.590
or someone has maybe gone from pseudo code

00:11:36.590 --> 00:11:39.423
to a new implementation of the algorithm.

00:11:40.270 --> 00:11:42.160
And finally, the lower right corner

00:11:42.160 --> 00:11:45.050
is really what we're aiming
for in the long-term right?

00:11:45.050 --> 00:11:47.920
Is to have generalizable results,

00:11:47.920 --> 00:11:50.940
so if you re implement the
method, redo the analysis

00:11:50.940 --> 00:11:54.970
using other data that
you get the same result

00:11:54.970 --> 00:11:57.490
that's really this
notion of generalization

00:11:57.490 --> 00:11:58.450
that we aim for,

00:11:58.450 --> 00:12:01.510
that's the long-term goal

00:12:01.510 --> 00:12:04.220
but for today I will talk
mostly about reproducibility

00:12:04.220 --> 00:12:07.513
as being one step towards
that longer term goal.

00:12:09.050 --> 00:12:10.430
Most of my work of course

00:12:10.430 --> 00:12:12.330
is in the field of reinforcement learning.

00:12:12.330 --> 00:12:13.260
From what I understand

00:12:13.260 --> 00:12:15.890
you already had a day of amazing lectures

00:12:15.890 --> 00:12:18.030
on reinforcement learning yesterday,

00:12:18.030 --> 00:12:21.480
so I'm not going to get
back into the basics,

00:12:21.480 --> 00:12:22.750
but just to ground us

00:12:22.750 --> 00:12:24.040
this is really the setting

00:12:24.040 --> 00:12:26.503
in which most of my work has been done.

00:12:27.350 --> 00:12:31.170
And that setting aside really
impressive success in games,

00:12:31.170 --> 00:12:32.900
that's where we've seen it shine

00:12:32.900 --> 00:12:35.190
and it's made the headlines,

00:12:35.190 --> 00:12:39.250
but it's useful to remember
that beyond those games

00:12:39.250 --> 00:12:40.690
where reinforcement learning

00:12:40.690 --> 00:12:43.550
has had some very visible success,

00:12:43.550 --> 00:12:46.980
there's a long list of potential
real world applications

00:12:46.980 --> 00:12:49.600
where reinforcement learning
could really be a game changer

00:12:49.600 --> 00:12:53.480
in our ability to develop
some high-quality technology.

00:12:53.480 --> 00:12:57.420
I mentioned a few of them
in the opening of the talk,

00:12:57.420 --> 00:13:00.270
but there's actually a much longer list

00:13:00.270 --> 00:13:01.860
that is under investigation

00:13:01.860 --> 00:13:04.770
including some assisted robotic devices,

00:13:04.770 --> 00:13:07.490
including some personalized
tutoring system,

00:13:07.490 --> 00:13:10.710
forest fire management, and on and on.

00:13:10.710 --> 00:13:11.920
And so, in a sense,

00:13:11.920 --> 00:13:14.150
I'm not so concerned about reproducibility

00:13:14.150 --> 00:13:16.180
for building systems that can play go,

00:13:16.180 --> 00:13:17.500
or that can play poker.

00:13:17.500 --> 00:13:21.690
I'm really concerned about
having strong reproducibility

00:13:21.690 --> 00:13:23.570
for these types of applications

00:13:23.570 --> 00:13:27.340
that are going to go into
everyday life of people.

00:13:27.340 --> 00:13:29.310
In some cases may be playing a major role

00:13:29.310 --> 00:13:32.210
in their health, in their
education, in their environment.

00:13:34.060 --> 00:13:36.130
Let me take a pause
there before digging in,

00:13:36.130 --> 00:13:39.250
and there's some questions about
some of the basic concepts,

00:13:39.250 --> 00:13:43.100
and then I'll launch into the next piece.

00:13:43.100 --> 00:13:45.230
Do we have any questions, Liam?

00:13:45.230 --> 00:13:46.750
- Yeah, we have a few.

00:13:46.750 --> 00:13:48.977
So Florian Gables asks,

00:13:48.977 --> 00:13:52.540
"How is reproducibly affected by time?

00:13:52.540 --> 00:13:55.570
I expect older papers tend
to be more famous papers

00:13:55.570 --> 00:13:57.890
and thus are more likely
to be reproducible,

00:13:57.890 --> 00:13:59.140
also in the last 20 years,

00:13:59.140 --> 00:14:02.360
the ML field got away
from more competitive

00:14:02.360 --> 00:14:04.257
causing more bad actors?"

00:14:05.820 --> 00:14:06.730
Exactly right.

00:14:06.730 --> 00:14:08.790
- Yeah, I think there's different factors,

00:14:08.790 --> 00:14:11.310
we haven't done a timing analysis of it,

00:14:11.310 --> 00:14:14.010
there's some things that
are easier from the past,

00:14:14.010 --> 00:14:16.200
you know, computation
is rarely an obstacle,

00:14:16.200 --> 00:14:17.990
we have so much more computation now

00:14:17.990 --> 00:14:20.103
than we did 10 years ago.

00:14:21.410 --> 00:14:25.100
In many cases, the types
of tasks that were done

00:14:25.100 --> 00:14:27.920
that were attempted in those
earlier papers were simpler

00:14:27.920 --> 00:14:31.070
and therefore by its nature
really easier to reproduce.

00:14:31.070 --> 00:14:33.130
One thing that's harder is in many cases,

00:14:33.130 --> 00:14:34.960
we don't have code from the early days,

00:14:34.960 --> 00:14:37.350
whereas it's now become
much more common for people

00:14:37.350 --> 00:14:39.410
to open source their code.

00:14:39.410 --> 00:14:42.750
We also often don't have the same ability

00:14:42.750 --> 00:14:44.330
to contact the authors,

00:14:44.330 --> 00:14:46.300
and so some people have
moved out of research

00:14:46.300 --> 00:14:47.280
and so on and so forth,

00:14:47.280 --> 00:14:50.140
and so it's really not as
easy to get into the details,

00:14:50.140 --> 00:14:52.853
so there's some of these
factors that come into play.

00:14:54.120 --> 00:14:56.810
- Related question about publishing code,

00:14:56.810 --> 00:14:59.767
I guess it's a personal asking
for your personal opinion.

00:14:59.767 --> 00:15:01.770
"Do you think that publishing
code of implementations

00:15:01.770 --> 00:15:05.937
should be compulsory for
publishing research?"

00:15:09.480 --> 00:15:12.880
- I intend to be like not one
that dwells with absolute,

00:15:12.880 --> 00:15:15.850
so I'm not going to go so
far as being compulsory,

00:15:15.850 --> 00:15:19.160
I will say, I think it's
absolutely a responsibility

00:15:19.160 --> 00:15:20.900
of anyone who engages in science

00:15:20.900 --> 00:15:22.980
to be transparent in doing that.

00:15:22.980 --> 00:15:25.900
So for me, publishing code is absolutely

00:15:26.890 --> 00:15:28.340
a first step towards this,

00:15:28.340 --> 00:15:31.420
I will say, even for
people working in industry,

00:15:31.420 --> 00:15:33.750
if you're engaging in research

00:15:33.750 --> 00:15:36.740
then publishing your
code should be expected.

00:15:36.740 --> 00:15:38.020
On the side of data

00:15:38.020 --> 00:15:40.840
I think it's a lot more nuance
how we think about that,

00:15:40.840 --> 00:15:43.120
there are many reasons why
data needs to be treated

00:15:43.120 --> 00:15:45.920
with appropriate confidentiality,
privacy standards,

00:15:45.920 --> 00:15:48.930
whether it's healthcare data,
identifiable data and so on.

00:15:48.930 --> 00:15:52.173
So there, I think we need a
much more nuanced discussion.

00:15:54.420 --> 00:15:56.690
- Okay, great, and point
of clarification maybe

00:15:56.690 --> 00:16:00.510
from the last slide on the
difference between replicability

00:16:00.510 --> 00:16:02.023
and generalizability.

00:16:03.450 --> 00:16:06.340
- Yeah, just going back
quickly to this one, right?

00:16:06.340 --> 00:16:07.920
In replicability and again,

00:16:07.920 --> 00:16:11.740
I'm really using a framework
that's proposed by NSF.

00:16:11.740 --> 00:16:14.930
In replicability we
assume that the data set

00:16:14.930 --> 00:16:17.190
is not necessarily the same,

00:16:17.190 --> 00:16:20.123
but that the code and
the analysis is the same.

00:16:21.312 --> 00:16:22.420
In generalizability,

00:16:22.420 --> 00:16:23.640
you may actually have

00:16:23.640 --> 00:16:25.530
slightly different methods for the code,

00:16:25.530 --> 00:16:27.550
for the analysis may be
different storage system,

00:16:27.550 --> 00:16:30.220
maybe different optimizer that's used

00:16:30.220 --> 00:16:33.730
and so the results are in a
sense assumed to be, you know,

00:16:33.730 --> 00:16:37.683
much more robust and
generalizable to other cases.

00:16:39.430 --> 00:16:42.220
So the idea is really robust to variance

00:16:42.220 --> 00:16:44.433
of both data and code in analysis.

00:16:47.160 --> 00:16:50.333
- Okay, stop me when you feel
like we've done enough, but--

00:16:51.400 --> 00:16:53.340
- Like last one and then
we'll get back to you.

00:16:53.340 --> 00:16:55.790
- Okay, do you know if
there are any suggestions

00:16:55.790 --> 00:16:58.563
on how to write
reproducible research code?

00:16:59.990 --> 00:17:02.500
- We'll get to some of
that at the later phase,

00:17:02.500 --> 00:17:07.500
and then I will probably
suggest some specific tools.

00:17:07.860 --> 00:17:09.210
I think in many cases,

00:17:09.210 --> 00:17:11.970
one of the things we can do to help us

00:17:11.970 --> 00:17:15.260
is to have tooling and best
practices and guidelines

00:17:15.260 --> 00:17:17.450
of how to do with that
so it's not general.

00:17:17.450 --> 00:17:19.130
So in the last part of my talk,

00:17:19.130 --> 00:17:21.770
I'll get into more specifics
in terms of tooling.

00:17:21.770 --> 00:17:24.883
Both for code and more
generally for experiments.

00:17:26.460 --> 00:17:27.293
- Okay, great.

00:17:28.200 --> 00:17:30.510
- Let's tell a short part of the story

00:17:30.510 --> 00:17:33.050
and we'll pause again for
questions maybe in 20 minutes

00:17:33.050 --> 00:17:34.350
or so and also at the end.

00:17:35.260 --> 00:17:38.750
So I mentioned that I have a
deep interest in applications

00:17:38.750 --> 00:17:41.290
and this is where this is
motivated in many ways,

00:17:41.290 --> 00:17:45.490
and so in some of the
earlier work that we've done,

00:17:45.490 --> 00:17:48.850
we looked at developing
a neurostimulation device

00:17:48.850 --> 00:17:50.270
for epilepsy.

00:17:50.270 --> 00:17:52.640
So think of this as a small device

00:17:52.640 --> 00:17:54.030
implanted in the chest cavity

00:17:54.030 --> 00:17:57.210
that has electrodes
running into the brain,

00:17:57.210 --> 00:17:58.350
some of these electrodes

00:17:58.350 --> 00:18:01.040
are in real time reading the signal,

00:18:01.040 --> 00:18:04.260
that EEG signal from the
brain from the patient.

00:18:04.260 --> 00:18:05.854
And some of the other electrodes

00:18:05.854 --> 00:18:10.840
are actually giving us electrical signal

00:18:10.840 --> 00:18:13.860
in the form of periodic stimulation

00:18:13.860 --> 00:18:17.700
to essentially disrupt the
neural signal in the brain

00:18:17.700 --> 00:18:22.700
and the principle being that
if you disrupt the signal,

00:18:23.340 --> 00:18:24.980
the brain doesn't move

00:18:24.980 --> 00:18:27.700
into the state of hyper synchronization.

00:18:27.700 --> 00:18:30.010
So epilepsy happens when all the neurons

00:18:30.010 --> 00:18:32.420
suddenly decide to fire together,

00:18:32.420 --> 00:18:34.750
but by adding external signal,

00:18:34.750 --> 00:18:38.140
you're disrupting that
synchronization ability,

00:18:38.140 --> 00:18:39.670
and that means that you can prevent

00:18:39.670 --> 00:18:41.520
epileptic seizure in patients.

00:18:41.520 --> 00:18:44.160
So these devices are in use in patients,

00:18:44.160 --> 00:18:47.100
they stimulate different
areas of the brain

00:18:47.100 --> 00:18:48.400
depending on the patients,

00:18:50.575 --> 00:18:53.940
most of them are operating on
an open loop control system.

00:18:53.940 --> 00:18:57.410
So they're just stimulating
at a particular frequency

00:18:57.410 --> 00:19:00.490
without considering what
is the real time activities

00:19:00.490 --> 00:19:03.070
so there's essentially
no sensing electrode.

00:19:03.070 --> 00:19:04.450
And so in our work,

00:19:04.450 --> 00:19:06.990
we really worked at using
reinforcement learning

00:19:06.990 --> 00:19:10.580
to improve the stimulation strategy

00:19:10.580 --> 00:19:13.670
in a way that we can reduce
the frequency of seizures,

00:19:13.670 --> 00:19:17.410
the duration, and also reduce
the amount of stimulation

00:19:17.410 --> 00:19:20.900
that's provided compared
to periodic strategy.

00:19:20.900 --> 00:19:22.070
And so in this case, you know,

00:19:22.070 --> 00:19:25.850
the signal we're reading
is a live EEG signal,

00:19:25.850 --> 00:19:27.660
and in this case

00:19:27.660 --> 00:19:30.020
we're really trying to
deliver the stimulation,

00:19:30.020 --> 00:19:32.700
so here you see a case
of periodic stimulation

00:19:32.700 --> 00:19:34.460
where every second the system

00:19:34.460 --> 00:19:36.633
was essentially poked electrically.

00:19:37.710 --> 00:19:41.170
There was already evidence
that this stimulation

00:19:41.170 --> 00:19:44.290
at a periodic pattern was effective

00:19:44.290 --> 00:19:48.190
in disturbing the patterns
of the hyper synchronization,

00:19:48.190 --> 00:19:49.800
so preventing seizures,

00:19:49.800 --> 00:19:53.030
but there hadn't been any work
on using adaptive learning

00:19:53.030 --> 00:19:56.650
to improve the strategy
in terms of stimulation.

00:19:56.650 --> 00:19:59.040
So that was the essence of our project.

00:19:59.040 --> 00:20:02.530
And at some point later on
share much more of the details

00:20:02.530 --> 00:20:03.410
of this experiments,

00:20:03.410 --> 00:20:06.340
but it's sort of just set the
stage with a kind of safety

00:20:06.340 --> 00:20:09.260
critical application
that I'm dealing with.

00:20:09.260 --> 00:20:12.530
All of our work was done in
an animal model of epilepsy,

00:20:12.530 --> 00:20:16.420
and so this is a slice
of the brain of a rat

00:20:16.420 --> 00:20:19.200
that is put in a particular solution

00:20:19.200 --> 00:20:22.710
where we can plant the electrodes
and observe the seizures,

00:20:22.710 --> 00:20:24.000
in this case the seizures

00:20:24.000 --> 00:20:26.390
are induced by a particular chemical

00:20:26.390 --> 00:20:27.800
that's put in the solution

00:20:27.800 --> 00:20:30.283
rather than a naturally epileptic animal.

00:20:31.280 --> 00:20:32.530
And in that system,

00:20:32.530 --> 00:20:34.210
we literally come in with a laptop

00:20:34.210 --> 00:20:36.970
and plug in our stimulation electrode

00:20:36.970 --> 00:20:40.750
and our sensing electrode as
input an output of that laptop,

00:20:40.750 --> 00:20:44.663
and we run a closed loop
reinforcement learning system.

00:20:45.790 --> 00:20:47.190
And so when you can imagine, you know,

00:20:47.190 --> 00:20:49.880
whatever results we get in
this particular animal model,

00:20:49.880 --> 00:20:53.410
we want to make sure that
they're actually transferable

00:20:53.410 --> 00:20:56.670
to other live animal or humans cases.

00:20:56.670 --> 00:21:00.520
And so this standard of
reproducibility is really motivated

00:21:00.520 --> 00:21:02.870
by making sure that the
experiments I conduct

00:21:02.870 --> 00:21:05.680
in the simpler setting
are really reliable enough

00:21:05.680 --> 00:21:08.613
to know whether we should
move to the other settings.

00:21:09.570 --> 00:21:11.340
And again, you know, if you want to go in,

00:21:11.340 --> 00:21:13.040
read about some of the work we've done

00:21:13.040 --> 00:21:15.160
that we've published a number
of papers on this topic

00:21:15.160 --> 00:21:17.750
that the conclusion of it
was that after several months

00:21:17.750 --> 00:21:20.820
of investigation this was
really a three year study,

00:21:20.820 --> 00:21:22.780
we were able to show that

00:21:22.780 --> 00:21:26.410
we could have as good of
a reduction of seizures

00:21:26.410 --> 00:21:28.360
compared to some of
the periodic strategies

00:21:28.360 --> 00:21:33.360
with many fewer actual
stimulation actions,

00:21:33.550 --> 00:21:34.960
so that was quite positive.

00:21:34.960 --> 00:21:37.460
But if you look at the
fine print at the bottom,

00:21:37.460 --> 00:21:40.780
we spent about six months
building up the training data,

00:21:40.780 --> 00:21:43.950
we spent about six months
training the algorithm,

00:21:43.950 --> 00:21:46.110
but then just the validation phase,

00:21:46.110 --> 00:21:48.750
so running the experiments to confirm

00:21:48.750 --> 00:21:52.030
that the RL trained
strategy was doing better

00:21:52.030 --> 00:21:54.500
than the baseline took two years.

00:21:54.500 --> 00:21:56.320
So imagine you're running an experiment,

00:21:56.320 --> 00:21:57.257
you essentially get one experiment

00:21:57.257 --> 00:21:59.440
that's going to take
two years and at the end

00:21:59.440 --> 00:22:02.600
you will know whether your
algorithm works or not.

00:22:02.600 --> 00:22:05.500
So thinking back to that pipeline

00:22:05.500 --> 00:22:08.780
I had the experimental
pipeline on slide two or three,

00:22:08.780 --> 00:22:12.100
you know, that phase of
going back and improving,

00:22:12.100 --> 00:22:14.910
you only get to do it after
essentially three years

00:22:14.910 --> 00:22:16.370
in this case.

00:22:16.370 --> 00:22:18.150
And so the stakes are very, very high

00:22:18.150 --> 00:22:20.630
that whatever you've done
in the initial phases

00:22:20.630 --> 00:22:22.640
actually have high quality enough

00:22:22.640 --> 00:22:24.330
and then you sort of run your experiment,

00:22:24.330 --> 00:22:26.530
but cross your fingers
that it's going to work.

00:22:27.530 --> 00:22:29.410
In our case, fortunately

00:22:29.410 --> 00:22:32.420
we did have promising enough results

00:22:32.420 --> 00:22:34.513
to continue to publish,

00:22:35.830 --> 00:22:38.030
but that wasn't necessarily
going to be a second try,

00:22:38.030 --> 00:22:39.250
and it's quite different

00:22:39.250 --> 00:22:41.620
than a lot of the machine
learning experiments

00:22:41.620 --> 00:22:43.780
that are being done today,

00:22:43.780 --> 00:22:45.620
we don't do all of our work in this mode,

00:22:45.620 --> 00:22:47.660
I have lots of other work where, you know,

00:22:47.660 --> 00:22:49.760
you launch an experiment,
you go grab a coffee,

00:22:49.760 --> 00:22:52.330
40 minutes later you have your results.

00:22:52.330 --> 00:22:54.500
But I just wanted to bring to your mind

00:22:54.500 --> 00:22:56.220
sort of the different cases,

00:22:56.220 --> 00:22:58.880
depending on what the type
of application you're doing,

00:22:58.880 --> 00:23:01.510
and you're trying to really solve,

00:23:01.510 --> 00:23:03.390
it may be very different setting,

00:23:03.390 --> 00:23:06.370
and there may be cases
where it's really important

00:23:06.370 --> 00:23:07.410
that you up your game

00:23:07.410 --> 00:23:09.160
in terms of the quality
of your experiments

00:23:09.160 --> 00:23:11.110
otherwise you're essentially
losing three years

00:23:11.110 --> 00:23:12.800
worth of work as well as the ability

00:23:12.800 --> 00:23:15.173
to have a positive
health impact on people.

00:23:16.020 --> 00:23:19.090
And so this is one of the
cases that's motivated me.

00:23:19.090 --> 00:23:22.100
When you take on one of these
applications, of course,

00:23:22.100 --> 00:23:22.933
as you can imagine,

00:23:22.933 --> 00:23:25.100
you can't afford to just try out

00:23:25.100 --> 00:23:26.620
all the algorithms out there

00:23:26.620 --> 00:23:29.210
and really figure out
through trial and error

00:23:29.210 --> 00:23:30.510
what works and what doesn't work.

00:23:30.510 --> 00:23:33.410
We didn't have a simulator
for this particular case,

00:23:33.410 --> 00:23:36.010
we had offline batch data to train from,

00:23:36.010 --> 00:23:38.330
we weren't even able to train online.

00:23:38.330 --> 00:23:39.760
And so there's a lot of constraints

00:23:39.760 --> 00:23:43.330
that are imposed by the
setting in which you work.

00:23:43.330 --> 00:23:44.810
And so we looked at the literature,

00:23:44.810 --> 00:23:46.690
the set of papers that were available

00:23:46.690 --> 00:23:48.879
to try to pick the methods that we thought

00:23:48.879 --> 00:23:50.900
was the most promising one.

00:23:50.900 --> 00:23:51.733
And as you can see,

00:23:51.733 --> 00:23:55.250
there's a huge number of papers
published every year on RL.

00:23:55.250 --> 00:23:57.890
And if each of these paper doesn't adopt

00:23:57.890 --> 00:23:59.330
a really good methodology,

00:23:59.330 --> 00:24:03.140
no matter how good I am
in setting up my own work,

00:24:03.140 --> 00:24:06.160
if each of these papers on
which I'm basing my work,

00:24:06.160 --> 00:24:08.580
doesn't use as good of a methodology,

00:24:08.580 --> 00:24:10.430
I'm a little bit stuck
because I have to redo

00:24:10.430 --> 00:24:12.110
all of their experiments before

00:24:12.110 --> 00:24:14.883
even knowing how to set
up my own experiments.

00:24:16.190 --> 00:24:19.040
And so in this case, you know,

00:24:19.040 --> 00:24:22.290
I'm going to focus on a
particular study we did

00:24:22.290 --> 00:24:24.810
trying to compare some of these algorithms

00:24:24.810 --> 00:24:26.020
in the literature.

00:24:26.020 --> 00:24:28.620
This is a little bit separate
from the epilepsy case,

00:24:28.620 --> 00:24:30.630
we didn't do this insimulation,

00:24:30.630 --> 00:24:33.420
but we were really trying
to dig into this question

00:24:33.420 --> 00:24:35.360
a little bit similar to the article

00:24:35.360 --> 00:24:36.670
from Edward Raff earlier

00:24:36.670 --> 00:24:39.160
but in a much more restrictive setting

00:24:39.160 --> 00:24:42.570
of policy search methods for RL,

00:24:42.570 --> 00:24:46.270
what was the quality of
reproducibility of these methods?

00:24:46.270 --> 00:24:49.960
And so we picked four
algorithms listed here,

00:24:49.960 --> 00:24:51.380
and the reason we picked them frankly

00:24:51.380 --> 00:24:54.350
is because they were
used often as baseline

00:24:54.350 --> 00:24:55.370
across the literature,

00:24:55.370 --> 00:24:57.990
so over and over again
we'd come with people

00:24:57.990 --> 00:24:59.950
presenting new methods for RL,

00:24:59.950 --> 00:25:01.810
but they were comparing their new methods

00:25:01.810 --> 00:25:05.180
to one or a few of these existing papers,

00:25:05.180 --> 00:25:08.810
so they'd become really a
reference set for the field.

00:25:08.810 --> 00:25:12.000
And we wanted to know are
they a good reference set?

00:25:12.000 --> 00:25:14.070
If your baselines aren't the right one,

00:25:14.070 --> 00:25:15.270
whatever you compare to it,

00:25:15.270 --> 00:25:18.200
it's going to be hard to
draw the right conclusions.

00:25:18.200 --> 00:25:20.290
And we had a suspicion

00:25:20.290 --> 00:25:23.610
that perhaps the way people
were using these algorithms

00:25:23.610 --> 00:25:25.080
wasn't the right way

00:25:25.080 --> 00:25:28.560
in terms of establishing
reproducible results.

00:25:28.560 --> 00:25:30.180
So let me tell you a
little bit what happened

00:25:30.180 --> 00:25:31.550
when we did this.

00:25:31.550 --> 00:25:35.240
We consider the four algorithms
and this is not in any way

00:25:35.240 --> 00:25:37.600
a critique necessarily
of these four algorithm,

00:25:37.600 --> 00:25:40.060
it's really trying to
dig into this question

00:25:40.060 --> 00:25:42.760
of how to run good experiments.

00:25:42.760 --> 00:25:44.340
And so I'm not necessarily
going to tell you

00:25:44.340 --> 00:25:45.320
which is better or not,

00:25:45.320 --> 00:25:50.290
so don't base your own analysis on this.

00:25:50.290 --> 00:25:52.320
But we consider this cheat on domain.

00:25:52.320 --> 00:25:54.980
It's a classic of the Mujuco literature,

00:25:54.980 --> 00:25:58.430
you essentially have a
simplified animated agent

00:25:58.430 --> 00:25:59.320
learning to run,

00:25:59.320 --> 00:26:01.140
so what you're seeing on this short video

00:26:01.140 --> 00:26:03.120
is what happens when you were successful

00:26:03.120 --> 00:26:06.750
in training your half cheetah
in learning how to run.

00:26:06.750 --> 00:26:08.390
We applied the four algorithms,

00:26:08.390 --> 00:26:09.350
as you can see, you know,

00:26:09.350 --> 00:26:12.523
algorithm red turned out to be
better than the other three.

00:26:13.470 --> 00:26:15.170
We then move to other cases,

00:26:15.170 --> 00:26:17.150
there's a hopper and there's a swimmer,

00:26:17.150 --> 00:26:20.040
and they're all using
the same kind of physics

00:26:20.040 --> 00:26:22.680
and an observation.

00:26:22.680 --> 00:26:24.310
And when we moved to these other ones,

00:26:24.310 --> 00:26:26.400
we found that in hopper,
the blue algorithm,

00:26:26.400 --> 00:26:29.500
which had been worse from
cheetah was actually the best.

00:26:29.500 --> 00:26:31.710
When we moved to swim or
blue was doing really well,

00:26:31.710 --> 00:26:35.270
again, but in that case
was really unstable,

00:26:35.270 --> 00:26:37.670
and you see that by the
very, very large error bands

00:26:37.670 --> 00:26:38.770
that are shown.

00:26:38.770 --> 00:26:41.970
Whereas red was essentially
unable to learn.

00:26:41.970 --> 00:26:43.680
So this is a little troubling

00:26:43.680 --> 00:26:45.850
because those three environments

00:26:45.850 --> 00:26:48.640
are actually operating
on very similar dynamics.

00:26:48.640 --> 00:26:51.360
And so anything I want
to conclude from this

00:26:51.360 --> 00:26:55.130
and take to doing a project
such as my epilepsy,

00:26:55.130 --> 00:26:57.000
you know, stimulation system,

00:26:57.000 --> 00:26:59.580
it's very hard to do when
even comparing a cost

00:26:59.580 --> 00:27:01.830
across three, very, very
similar environments,

00:27:01.830 --> 00:27:03.723
you get very different results.

00:27:04.690 --> 00:27:06.620
So we started digging in a little bit

00:27:06.620 --> 00:27:10.300
to understand what was behind
this really high variance

00:27:10.300 --> 00:27:12.400
between the results.

00:27:12.400 --> 00:27:15.490
We thought maybe we are
not getting the right code,

00:27:15.490 --> 00:27:17.810
so, you know, one of the
reasons we picked these

00:27:17.810 --> 00:27:19.070
is also because there was a lot

00:27:19.070 --> 00:27:20.560
of open source implementation,

00:27:20.560 --> 00:27:24.440
we had grabbed one that was
perhaps the best known one.

00:27:24.440 --> 00:27:28.050
We went digging around and
found lots of other repos

00:27:28.050 --> 00:27:31.620
trying to implement the same
algorithm and what we found

00:27:31.620 --> 00:27:34.280
is if you take three different
implementation of TRPA,

00:27:34.280 --> 00:27:36.410
which should be the same algorithm,

00:27:36.410 --> 00:27:39.810
you get three results of this type.

00:27:39.810 --> 00:27:41.010
And so if you'll go back to,

00:27:41.010 --> 00:27:43.820
you know, my topology of robustness,

00:27:43.820 --> 00:27:45.970
replicability, reproducibility, and so on,

00:27:45.970 --> 00:27:49.110
you see what happens in
this case where same data,

00:27:49.110 --> 00:27:51.380
different code based implementation,

00:27:51.380 --> 00:27:55.300
and you see quickly that
the results are changing.

00:27:55.300 --> 00:27:58.980
That's a little bit surprising,
but it's not unique to TRPO,

00:27:58.980 --> 00:28:00.970
we ran the same thing with DDPG

00:28:00.970 --> 00:28:03.613
and had similarly very different results.

00:28:04.660 --> 00:28:06.600
We were not going to be stopped there,

00:28:06.600 --> 00:28:07.970
you know, a big part of research

00:28:07.970 --> 00:28:09.930
is just trying to understand,

00:28:09.930 --> 00:28:12.610
and just like following
the piece of the story

00:28:12.610 --> 00:28:14.190
that you don't understand,

00:28:14.190 --> 00:28:15.720
and so, you know, in many ways

00:28:15.720 --> 00:28:20.090
that's one of the good
qualities as a researcher.

00:28:20.090 --> 00:28:22.730
So we started digging
into different reasons

00:28:22.730 --> 00:28:25.390
that might explain the variation.

00:28:25.390 --> 00:28:29.070
One of them that jumped out
is the network structure,

00:28:29.070 --> 00:28:32.260
so depending how you were
setting up the neural network

00:28:32.260 --> 00:28:33.860
that was representing your policy,

00:28:33.860 --> 00:28:36.000
you had very different results.

00:28:36.000 --> 00:28:37.730
We changed the number of layers,

00:28:37.730 --> 00:28:39.090
the number of nodes per layer,

00:28:39.090 --> 00:28:41.870
as you can see lots of different results.

00:28:41.870 --> 00:28:44.010
We changed the activation function

00:28:44.010 --> 00:28:48.490
that also caused the quite
a few different results.

00:28:48.490 --> 00:28:51.750
We started digging into all
of these factors of variation

00:28:51.750 --> 00:28:55.390
and found out many ways they vary jointly,

00:28:55.390 --> 00:28:58.720
so we looked at the reward scale.

00:28:58.720 --> 00:29:02.200
So in some cases, people
normalize the reward itself,

00:29:02.200 --> 00:29:04.330
the reward function,
but then in other cases,

00:29:04.330 --> 00:29:07.130
people apply normalization at each layer

00:29:07.130 --> 00:29:08.530
of the neural network.

00:29:08.530 --> 00:29:10.220
So there's definitely an interaction

00:29:10.220 --> 00:29:11.983
between these two factors.

00:29:13.040 --> 00:29:16.440
Really at the end of the day,
there was such a large number

00:29:16.440 --> 00:29:20.910
of these experimental factors to consider

00:29:20.910 --> 00:29:24.020
that that explained most of
the variation we were seeing

00:29:24.020 --> 00:29:26.313
between these algorithms
and these methods.

00:29:27.400 --> 00:29:28.750
So some of this is reassuring

00:29:28.750 --> 00:29:31.030
because we understand
it's not black magic.

00:29:31.030 --> 00:29:33.620
We understand what are the
pieces that are affecting this.

00:29:33.620 --> 00:29:36.923
At the same time, there's a
large number of these pieces,

00:29:37.835 --> 00:29:40.970
and we also observed a bias

00:29:40.970 --> 00:29:43.130
in the way that the researchers

00:29:43.130 --> 00:29:46.460
were sending their experiment,
in that the amount of work

00:29:46.460 --> 00:29:50.050
that went into finding
the optimal configuration

00:29:50.050 --> 00:29:53.180
of these parameter for one's own method,

00:29:53.180 --> 00:29:55.620
wasn't really the same investment

00:29:55.620 --> 00:29:58.510
that one was putting into
finding the best hyper parameter

00:29:58.510 --> 00:30:00.630
for the baseline algorithm.

00:30:00.630 --> 00:30:01.710
So I assume, you know,

00:30:01.710 --> 00:30:04.400
many of you have read a
lot of research papers

00:30:04.400 --> 00:30:06.470
in which you see, you know,
you're proposing a new method,

00:30:06.470 --> 00:30:08.810
you comparing to a bunch
of baseline methods.

00:30:08.810 --> 00:30:10.830
So really the tricky question is,

00:30:10.830 --> 00:30:12.490
are you going to be as motivated

00:30:12.490 --> 00:30:15.480
and spend as much time computation,

00:30:15.480 --> 00:30:18.510
finding the right setting for the method

00:30:18.510 --> 00:30:20.440
that you're proposing as you are

00:30:20.440 --> 00:30:23.060
for the method that you are comparing to,

00:30:23.060 --> 00:30:24.740
so previous methods in the literature

00:30:24.740 --> 00:30:27.310
and, you know, human
behavior and psychology

00:30:27.310 --> 00:30:30.530
being what it is, you know,
there's a strong suspicion

00:30:30.530 --> 00:30:34.270
that people often don't do
this in a systematic way.

00:30:34.270 --> 00:30:37.320
And while we might think that's
setting up a fair comparison

00:30:37.320 --> 00:30:40.470
between method A method B is
easy, same amount of data,

00:30:40.470 --> 00:30:43.290
same amount of computation we're done,

00:30:43.290 --> 00:30:45.700
in practice, this is not what is happening

00:30:45.700 --> 00:30:48.483
in a very large number of studies.

00:30:49.480 --> 00:30:52.760
Let me show you one example
that was particularly revealing,

00:30:52.760 --> 00:30:57.120
in this case, my students
ran two different methods,

00:30:57.120 --> 00:30:59.260
the blue and the orange,
they came back and said,

00:30:59.260 --> 00:31:01.690
okay, we were very tight

00:31:01.690 --> 00:31:03.780
in controlling all the
factors of variation,

00:31:03.780 --> 00:31:06.630
we ran five iterations of each,

00:31:06.630 --> 00:31:10.240
we're averaging the results,
reporting the standard error.

00:31:10.240 --> 00:31:12.283
And we get this difference, which suggest

00:31:12.283 --> 00:31:15.350
that the orange method is
better than the blue method.

00:31:15.350 --> 00:31:17.470
So, you know, this was after several weeks

00:31:17.470 --> 00:31:22.130
of this work on reproducibility,
we said, okay, interesting.

00:31:22.130 --> 00:31:24.630
So what are the two methods
you're comparing here?

00:31:25.860 --> 00:31:30.600
And it turns out those two
were exactly the same methods,

00:31:30.600 --> 00:31:34.780
same code in both cases,
same budgets, same data

00:31:34.780 --> 00:31:38.090
for finding the hyper
parameter configuration.

00:31:38.090 --> 00:31:39.490
The only thing that was different

00:31:39.490 --> 00:31:41.510
is the set of seeds that were used

00:31:41.510 --> 00:31:43.400
to run each of these experiments,

00:31:43.400 --> 00:31:45.970
and the seeds come in because
there's some randomization

00:31:45.970 --> 00:31:49.283
in some of the initial
conditions of the learner.

00:31:50.320 --> 00:31:53.680
And so this is really
troubling because in a sense,

00:31:53.680 --> 00:31:56.170
the same method, it seems
like there's an advantage

00:31:56.170 --> 00:31:59.950
to one when in fact it's not supported.

00:31:59.950 --> 00:32:01.320
So there was a little bit of luck

00:32:01.320 --> 00:32:04.290
in how the seeds split into those cases,

00:32:04.290 --> 00:32:06.360
but it's still very, very concerning

00:32:06.360 --> 00:32:07.990
in terms of our ability

00:32:07.990 --> 00:32:11.830
to draw a robust conclusion
from such an experiment.

00:32:11.830 --> 00:32:13.800
And so it was good to step back and think

00:32:13.800 --> 00:32:16.380
like how we actually measuring performance

00:32:16.380 --> 00:32:17.980
of these policies.

00:32:17.980 --> 00:32:21.260
In many cases, what we
show is that dark line

00:32:21.260 --> 00:32:24.200
is the average over the test trial.

00:32:24.200 --> 00:32:27.330
And then the colored bar around it

00:32:27.330 --> 00:32:30.630
is really the confidence interval.

00:32:30.630 --> 00:32:33.890
Many cases we look at a
95% confidence interval,,

00:32:33.890 --> 00:32:36.500
the main factor that we get to pick

00:32:36.500 --> 00:32:39.670
is how many experiments do we run?

00:32:39.670 --> 00:32:40.640
And so in the example,

00:32:40.640 --> 00:32:43.810
before we had run five
experiments of each,

00:32:43.810 --> 00:32:46.120
I asked my student, why did
you pick five experiment,

00:32:46.120 --> 00:32:48.760
is that the right numbers?
Should we pick more?

00:32:48.760 --> 00:32:51.290
I said, well, we looked in the literature

00:32:51.290 --> 00:32:54.530
and five seems to be like the
rough number of experiments

00:32:54.530 --> 00:32:57.270
that people are running,
if nothing else seems

00:32:57.270 --> 00:33:00.880
to be the mode the
experiments at the time,

00:33:00.880 --> 00:33:02.750
this was a few years ago.

00:33:02.750 --> 00:33:04.660
And so if you look at this, you know,

00:33:04.660 --> 00:33:06.900
there's a funny thing that's happening,

00:33:06.900 --> 00:33:09.060
this is the number of trials,

00:33:09.060 --> 00:33:11.160
three to nine, mean some experiments,

00:33:11.160 --> 00:33:13.540
some results were done
on three experiments,

00:33:13.540 --> 00:33:16.340
other results were done
on nine experiments.

00:33:16.340 --> 00:33:17.870
There's a funny thing that happened

00:33:17.870 --> 00:33:20.330
that some of the papers
were only reporting

00:33:20.330 --> 00:33:22.660
the top two or the top three results.

00:33:22.660 --> 00:33:24.510
So they may be running 100 experiments,

00:33:24.510 --> 00:33:26.860
but they were only going
to report the top two,

00:33:26.860 --> 00:33:29.160
top three, top five results.

00:33:29.160 --> 00:33:30.880
And that kind of strategy

00:33:30.880 --> 00:33:33.560
was sort of creeping into the community,

00:33:33.560 --> 00:33:36.730
and if you go today and look
at papers from this era,

00:33:36.730 --> 00:33:38.450
and sometimes maybe even papers,

00:33:38.450 --> 00:33:39.920
if today in reinforcement learning,

00:33:39.920 --> 00:33:42.830
be very, very careful
about how they set up

00:33:42.830 --> 00:33:45.800
this particular part of the experiment.

00:33:45.800 --> 00:33:46.900
Let me show you what happens

00:33:46.900 --> 00:33:49.420
when you only look at
the top three results.

00:33:49.420 --> 00:33:52.770
So this is a case where I
ran 10 different experiments,

00:33:52.770 --> 00:33:56.670
and let's say, I have a particular
baseline I want to beat.

00:33:56.670 --> 00:33:59.380
So if I run 10 experiment
and I look at the results,

00:33:59.380 --> 00:34:02.110
this doesn't seem very convincing.

00:34:02.110 --> 00:34:05.050
I didn't draw the average
in confidence integral

00:34:05.050 --> 00:34:06.730
but that doesn't seem very convincing

00:34:06.730 --> 00:34:10.010
that, you know, we have
substantial evidence

00:34:10.010 --> 00:34:11.510
that we can beat the baseline.

00:34:12.610 --> 00:34:15.630
If I just keep my three best experiments,

00:34:15.630 --> 00:34:16.980
this is what it looks like.

00:34:18.560 --> 00:34:22.620
So suddenly I have a very
strong, positive bias,

00:34:22.620 --> 00:34:26.170
and also the variance
appears much smaller.

00:34:26.170 --> 00:34:31.170
So if the baseline
computed properly the mean

00:34:31.380 --> 00:34:34.910
and the confidence interval
over several examples,

00:34:34.910 --> 00:34:36.980
but someone comes along and they decide

00:34:36.980 --> 00:34:39.390
to just show the top three results,

00:34:39.390 --> 00:34:43.080
in doing so they will have a
fairly strong, positive bias

00:34:43.080 --> 00:34:46.130
and a smaller variance,
and thus their method

00:34:46.130 --> 00:34:48.053
will start to seem advantageous.

00:34:49.968 --> 00:34:53.670
And so, unfortunately there's
many papers in the literature

00:34:53.670 --> 00:34:55.820
that have done this over the years.

00:34:55.820 --> 00:34:56.950
What's even more damaging

00:34:56.950 --> 00:35:00.510
is when a set of paper starts to do this,

00:35:00.510 --> 00:35:03.020
even people who are very motivated,

00:35:03.020 --> 00:35:05.070
in terms of having good reproducibility,

00:35:05.070 --> 00:35:08.370
which says you use the same analysis,

00:35:08.370 --> 00:35:10.830
we'll use this particular analysis method

00:35:10.830 --> 00:35:13.490
because it's the same one
as previous literature.

00:35:13.490 --> 00:35:18.240
And we really get into a
culture of poor experimentation,

00:35:18.240 --> 00:35:21.943
even though we should not be doing this.

00:35:24.330 --> 00:35:29.330
And so the take home from
this, I think is twofold.

00:35:30.740 --> 00:35:32.870
One of them is in reinforcement learning,

00:35:32.870 --> 00:35:35.740
there are many, many sources
of experimental variation,

00:35:35.740 --> 00:35:39.500
it's good to be of all of them,

00:35:39.500 --> 00:35:43.070
and to be thorough in deciding

00:35:43.070 --> 00:35:46.150
how you're going to control for them,

00:35:46.150 --> 00:35:49.490
do it in a way that is
fair for your methods.

00:35:49.490 --> 00:35:51.610
The second one is to be really thorough

00:35:51.610 --> 00:35:54.810
in how you report your results,

00:35:54.810 --> 00:35:58.710
and see 18 that were published
across the top conferences.

00:35:58.710 --> 00:36:03.710
And we found that even though
all the papers had experiment,

00:36:04.270 --> 00:36:08.780
used neural networks and
describe the hyper-parameters

00:36:08.780 --> 00:36:12.380
for the new algorithms
that they were proposing,

00:36:12.380 --> 00:36:14.280
in many ways on other things

00:36:14.280 --> 00:36:17.900
that we would strongly
recommend that they do.

00:36:17.900 --> 00:36:20.060
So, you know, one of them is making sure

00:36:20.060 --> 00:36:22.610
that you thoroughly document
all the hyper-parameters

00:36:22.610 --> 00:36:27.240
for the baseline methods, even
including a link to the code

00:36:27.240 --> 00:36:30.040
so that people can actually
reproduce your methods,

00:36:30.040 --> 00:36:32.210
specifying it, how you went about

00:36:32.210 --> 00:36:34.700
choosing the hyper parameters
for all the methods

00:36:34.700 --> 00:36:36.820
or algorithms in the paper,

00:36:36.820 --> 00:36:41.030
running an evaluation on
a proper holdout test set,

00:36:41.030 --> 00:36:45.550
and finally properly
running significance testing

00:36:45.550 --> 00:36:49.230
to support the conclusion
of your analysis.

00:36:49.230 --> 00:36:53.340
So these are all ingredients
that I think are very important

00:36:53.340 --> 00:36:55.960
when reporting experiments
in reinforcement learning.

00:36:55.960 --> 00:36:59.510
Also this applies to many other
fields of machine learning

00:36:59.510 --> 00:37:02.480
to keep in mind, where
I think in many cases,

00:37:02.480 --> 00:37:04.390
the community has fallen short,

00:37:04.390 --> 00:37:07.363
as we see by this really brief analysis.

00:37:08.580 --> 00:37:11.390
Let me pause here before
I jump into a few,

00:37:11.390 --> 00:37:13.470
you know, solutions tools and so on,

00:37:13.470 --> 00:37:14.920
let's take a couple of questions

00:37:14.920 --> 00:37:18.520
and then I'll do the last bit of a talk

00:37:18.520 --> 00:37:20.050
that is a little bit more practical.

00:37:20.050 --> 00:37:21.410
This is really raising the alarm,

00:37:21.410 --> 00:37:24.520
but I will provide you with
some practical suggestions

00:37:24.520 --> 00:37:26.563
to avoid some of these pitfalls, Liam?

00:37:27.460 --> 00:37:29.420
- Yeah, so there are
several questions there,

00:37:29.420 --> 00:37:32.910
like what can we do? So I
will skip those for now.

00:37:32.910 --> 00:37:33.743
- Pause on those.

00:37:33.743 --> 00:37:36.410
- Don't cover them in the
next 10 minutes, okay.

00:37:36.410 --> 00:37:39.320
So in my experience,

00:37:39.320 --> 00:37:41.180
I've noticed a general lack of sensitivity

00:37:41.180 --> 00:37:42.810
about the different test environments

00:37:42.810 --> 00:37:44.490
and the different inherent difficulties

00:37:44.490 --> 00:37:46.760
that some environments
have compared to others.

00:37:46.760 --> 00:37:48.960
Very often articles
that propose experiments

00:37:48.960 --> 00:37:50.830
in real environments
were compared to others

00:37:50.830 --> 00:37:52.700
to analyze the effectiveness
of the algorithm

00:37:52.700 --> 00:37:56.020
in small discreet and often
non-dynamic environments,

00:37:56.020 --> 00:37:57.660
how do you think we can
increase the attention

00:37:57.660 --> 00:37:59.700
of some researchers to
the large amount of work

00:37:59.700 --> 00:38:01.170
prior to adapting an algorithm

00:38:01.170 --> 00:38:03.813
from a toy problem to a real problem?

00:38:07.330 --> 00:38:08.740
- It's really a challenge,

00:38:08.740 --> 00:38:10.400
and we've seen this over and over again.

00:38:10.400 --> 00:38:14.080
I will say that there's a
relatively small number of people

00:38:14.080 --> 00:38:16.150
who are really willing
to get their hands dirty

00:38:16.150 --> 00:38:18.133
and work with real world data,

00:38:18.133 --> 00:38:21.050
I hope that many, many more people

00:38:21.050 --> 00:38:23.060
would be willing to do that.

00:38:23.060 --> 00:38:26.410
And I hope the upcoming
generation of researchers

00:38:26.410 --> 00:38:28.593
will really engage in this.

00:38:29.620 --> 00:38:32.990
You know, for me, there's
two really ingredients here,

00:38:32.990 --> 00:38:36.680
one of them is transparency
to be thorough and transparent

00:38:36.680 --> 00:38:39.780
in reporting the steps of your
research and the trade-offs

00:38:39.780 --> 00:38:42.210
and the limitations of
anything you've done.

00:38:42.210 --> 00:38:45.530
And secondly, it's really
entering always the work

00:38:45.530 --> 00:38:49.410
from a mindset of
understanding of inquisition,

00:38:49.410 --> 00:38:51.060
not trying to win any medals.

00:38:51.060 --> 00:38:52.680
This is not the Olympics,

00:38:52.680 --> 00:38:54.740
this is not how research is best done,

00:38:54.740 --> 00:38:56.510
but really the spirit of inquiry

00:38:56.510 --> 00:38:58.810
and of growing our knowledge together.

00:38:58.810 --> 00:39:00.300
And those two ingredients, I think

00:39:00.300 --> 00:39:01.860
if you keep that really top of mind

00:39:01.860 --> 00:39:04.463
are the guiding lights for
doing this kind of work.

00:39:07.100 --> 00:39:09.820
- Great, a question for Matthew McFarland

00:39:09.820 --> 00:39:11.100
that I think you partially answered

00:39:11.100 --> 00:39:12.300
in the last slides of your talk,

00:39:12.300 --> 00:39:14.900
but given the large variability in results

00:39:14.900 --> 00:39:17.130
for different implementations
of the same baseline,

00:39:17.130 --> 00:39:19.460
does this mean we have to be skeptical

00:39:19.460 --> 00:39:22.780
of all research we read,
how can we identify

00:39:22.780 --> 00:39:26.430
the papers that present
raw results we can rely on?

00:39:26.430 --> 00:39:29.193
- Yeah, you should always be skeptical,

00:39:30.120 --> 00:39:30.953
at the same time, you know,

00:39:30.953 --> 00:39:35.031
there's only so much energy you
can put on re-investigation.

00:39:35.031 --> 00:39:38.070
I do think as the community of research,

00:39:38.070 --> 00:39:41.140
we don't spend enough
time being skeptical,

00:39:41.140 --> 00:39:43.840
reproducing work, investing and testing

00:39:43.840 --> 00:39:45.970
the robustness of our knowledge,

00:39:45.970 --> 00:39:50.970
versus always, you know, there's a real,

00:39:50.992 --> 00:39:51.825
I'm not sure how to call it,

00:39:51.825 --> 00:39:55.290
but like a fetishizing of
novelty that I think is damaging

00:39:55.290 --> 00:39:58.080
to the quality of the research process.

00:39:58.080 --> 00:40:00.330
And so I think we need
to invest more in that

00:40:01.220 --> 00:40:03.390
and each of us needs to
carry a part of that,

00:40:03.390 --> 00:40:06.210
and I'll give some tips on
that coming up in a minute.

00:40:06.210 --> 00:40:08.220
Last question, maybe,
and then we'll conclude

00:40:08.220 --> 00:40:10.230
and take some of the others at the end.

00:40:10.230 --> 00:40:12.840
- Okay, great, so this is a
question from who, Cesarean,

00:40:12.840 --> 00:40:16.450
in social science research,
reproducibility is not possible

00:40:16.450 --> 00:40:19.510
because it varies very
dramatically over time.

00:40:19.510 --> 00:40:22.620
How can generalization
be taken into account

00:40:22.620 --> 00:40:24.123
in these settings?

00:40:25.710 --> 00:40:28.740
- Yeah, and as much as you know,

00:40:28.740 --> 00:40:33.740
I call for better behavior
in machine learning research,

00:40:34.800 --> 00:40:36.220
I think in some of the other fields,

00:40:36.220 --> 00:40:38.297
this is much, much harder to do.

00:40:38.297 --> 00:40:40.480
And so we produce stability
as strictly defined here,

00:40:40.480 --> 00:40:43.150
is as feasible as was
mentioned, you really,

00:40:43.150 --> 00:40:47.057
but generalized visibility
always have the ability

00:40:49.660 --> 00:40:51.000
to describe your approach

00:40:51.000 --> 00:40:54.173
and results in another lab to carry that.

00:40:56.250 --> 00:40:58.980
And so, in some cases, you
know, in clinical sciences,

00:40:58.980 --> 00:41:01.640
it's really common to do
multi-center regular protocol

00:41:01.640 --> 00:41:03.000
across several hospitals,

00:41:03.000 --> 00:41:06.280
because we know that whichever
hospital ran the study

00:41:06.280 --> 00:41:09.130
is a major factor in the
results when you do the analysis

00:41:09.130 --> 00:41:10.580
of, you know, what are the causal factors

00:41:10.580 --> 00:41:12.940
that explain the results,
the location is one.

00:41:12.940 --> 00:41:15.730
So then we set up a multicenter study.

00:41:15.730 --> 00:41:17.430
So I think there's some of this practice

00:41:17.430 --> 00:41:19.450
of that can be carried to other fields

00:41:19.450 --> 00:41:21.203
to help reduce that variance.

00:41:23.484 --> 00:41:25.710
Let me jump in with a few recommendation

00:41:25.710 --> 00:41:28.170
of where we're moving as
a field and as a community

00:41:28.170 --> 00:41:30.693
and I'll take a few last
questions at the end.

00:41:32.960 --> 00:41:35.210
You know, one thing I want to recognize

00:41:35.210 --> 00:41:37.370
is machine learning systems are complex,

00:41:37.370 --> 00:41:40.430
there's several aspects to consider

00:41:40.430 --> 00:41:43.680
when building our experiments,
whether it's the code,

00:41:43.680 --> 00:41:45.820
the implementation, the
machines were running on,

00:41:45.820 --> 00:41:49.830
the system, the data,
also the social systems

00:41:49.830 --> 00:41:51.743
in which we're embedding our work.

00:41:52.670 --> 00:41:56.023
And so we have to be
careful of considering that.

00:41:58.330 --> 00:41:59.910
Perhaps on that more optimistic side,

00:41:59.910 --> 00:42:04.060
you know, we have dealt with
many, many other complex system

00:42:04.060 --> 00:42:05.900
and I'm pointing out two of them here

00:42:05.900 --> 00:42:07.560
because they've been an inspiration

00:42:07.560 --> 00:42:10.050
to some of the solutions we've drawn.

00:42:10.050 --> 00:42:14.850
One of them is, you
know, flying an airplane

00:42:14.850 --> 00:42:17.310
was not always as safe as it is today,

00:42:17.310 --> 00:42:19.270
put yourself back 100 years,

00:42:19.270 --> 00:42:23.500
and really there was
a lot more uncertainty

00:42:23.500 --> 00:42:25.810
about the outcome of any plane ride,

00:42:25.810 --> 00:42:28.630
yet over time, the industry
has really raised its standards

00:42:28.630 --> 00:42:30.100
in terms of ensuring safety

00:42:30.100 --> 00:42:32.740
and it's remarkably more safe today

00:42:33.583 --> 00:42:35.197
than it was 100 years ago.

00:42:35.197 --> 00:42:38.960
And another case is really
what we're seeing in surgery

00:42:40.930 --> 00:42:42.110
very much the same thing.

00:42:42.110 --> 00:42:44.070
Surgery had been performed for centuries,

00:42:44.070 --> 00:42:46.470
but really the amount of care

00:42:46.470 --> 00:42:50.040
that goes into ensuring as safe procedures

00:42:50.930 --> 00:42:54.000
has really improved in
the last few decades.

00:42:54.000 --> 00:42:57.230
And they're both fields where
they've used very simple tools

00:42:57.230 --> 00:42:59.150
to raise that, you know,
there's been of course,

00:42:59.150 --> 00:43:01.520
amazing innovation at
the technology level,

00:43:01.520 --> 00:43:04.330
but one of the simplest tool
that they have used repeatedly

00:43:04.330 --> 00:43:07.880
is the use of checklists,
both in the cockpit

00:43:07.880 --> 00:43:09.580
and in the surgery room,

00:43:09.580 --> 00:43:12.890
a very prescriptive
checklist that the people,

00:43:12.890 --> 00:43:17.030
the actors go through while
engaging in their activity.

00:43:17.030 --> 00:43:20.600
And so in recent years,
we've proposed similarly,

00:43:20.600 --> 00:43:23.630
a machine learning
reproducibility checklist,

00:43:23.630 --> 00:43:27.021
it's available, it's a
set of different questions

00:43:27.021 --> 00:43:28.930
that you can run through
for your own work,

00:43:28.930 --> 00:43:32.220
that gives you high, your
certainty that you sort of checked

00:43:32.220 --> 00:43:36.210
all the possible sources of variation

00:43:36.210 --> 00:43:39.910
and with a goal to having
much more reproducible work.

00:43:39.910 --> 00:43:41.820
So there's five sections in the checklist,

00:43:41.820 --> 00:43:43.600
the first one is about all the models

00:43:43.600 --> 00:43:45.210
and the algorithms you present.

00:43:45.210 --> 00:43:47.990
The checklist is meant to be
used when you write a paper

00:43:47.990 --> 00:43:50.120
for a scientific conference or journal,

00:43:50.120 --> 00:43:52.740
but it can actually be
adopted much earlier on

00:43:52.740 --> 00:43:56.060
in the process of conducting
a research project.

00:43:56.060 --> 00:43:57.530
So for the models and the algorithms,

00:43:57.530 --> 00:43:59.650
you want to clearly
describe what you're doing,

00:43:59.650 --> 00:44:02.690
the assumptions, the
analysis of complexity

00:44:02.690 --> 00:44:04.940
for the theoretical claim,
you want to make sure

00:44:04.940 --> 00:44:08.020
you are stating it clearly
have a complete proof.

00:44:08.020 --> 00:44:11.330
For the dataset, there's
several factors to consider,

00:44:11.330 --> 00:44:14.390
and it's useful to be very
transparent about each of them,

00:44:14.390 --> 00:44:16.400
be able to describe them.

00:44:16.400 --> 00:44:19.730
For all the code that you want to share,

00:44:19.730 --> 00:44:22.310
again, a relatively
short list of ingredients

00:44:22.310 --> 00:44:24.410
that if you can include the dependencies,

00:44:24.410 --> 00:44:28.310
training code, evaluation
code, pre-training models,

00:44:28.310 --> 00:44:33.310
reading a file, it's
not really complicated,

00:44:33.410 --> 00:44:36.840
but you would be shocked at
how many papers published

00:44:36.840 --> 00:44:39.180
or they don't meet this basic standard.

00:44:39.180 --> 00:44:41.270
And for all the reported
experimental results,

00:44:41.270 --> 00:44:44.150
we also have a number of
items that we recommend,

00:44:44.150 --> 00:44:46.160
including and checking again,

00:44:46.160 --> 00:44:47.980
based on some of the analysis we did

00:44:47.980 --> 00:44:49.810
of what were the missing pieces

00:44:49.810 --> 00:44:52.630
in a purse in the current literature.

00:44:52.630 --> 00:44:54.710
And for both, you know,
some of the sections,

00:44:54.710 --> 00:44:56.330
in particular the code session,

00:44:56.330 --> 00:45:00.260
we did a lot of analysis
on GitHub repositories

00:45:00.260 --> 00:45:02.610
to see what was the correlation

00:45:02.610 --> 00:45:04.710
between whether people had included

00:45:04.710 --> 00:45:07.170
these really simple components or not,

00:45:07.170 --> 00:45:09.770
and how useful was the
code that we're doing.

00:45:09.770 --> 00:45:12.360
And we ran an interesting analysis,

00:45:12.360 --> 00:45:15.787
looking at of the five items that I listed

00:45:15.787 --> 00:45:18.490
the reproducibility checklist
if you had one, two, three,

00:45:18.490 --> 00:45:19.890
four or five mil clump,

00:45:19.890 --> 00:45:23.310
how that was correlated with
the number of GitHub stars

00:45:23.310 --> 00:45:25.780
that the repo would get.

00:45:25.780 --> 00:45:29.120
And we see that, you know,
packets that have all five ticks

00:45:29.120 --> 00:45:34.120
are much more likely to have a
large number of GitHub stars.

00:45:34.690 --> 00:45:38.220
So it seems relatively
simple in some ways,

00:45:38.220 --> 00:45:41.810
and yet to be really disciplined
and diligent about this

00:45:41.810 --> 00:45:43.250
is both going to have an impact

00:45:43.250 --> 00:45:45.970
in terms of people's
appreciation of your work

00:45:45.970 --> 00:45:49.140
and also the scientific
quality of your work.

00:45:49.140 --> 00:45:52.690
So this is a set of really
practical things that you can do.

00:45:52.690 --> 00:45:54.760
I will give you access to the slides,

00:45:54.760 --> 00:45:56.900
so you'll be able to dig
into all of the links,

00:45:56.900 --> 00:45:59.300
I do try to include on most of my slides,

00:45:59.300 --> 00:46:01.550
the relevant links to get more information

00:46:01.550 --> 00:46:03.003
about the particular topic.

00:46:04.110 --> 00:46:07.670
Beyond this we also ran a
reproducibility challenge

00:46:07.670 --> 00:46:08.740
for the last few years.

00:46:08.740 --> 00:46:11.230
So we've invited people to take a paper

00:46:11.230 --> 00:46:14.950
that was published at one
of these recent conferences.

00:46:14.950 --> 00:46:16.630
The challenge runs twice a year,

00:46:16.630 --> 00:46:19.430
all you have to do is
sort of pick up paper

00:46:19.430 --> 00:46:21.270
and set up to reproduce

00:46:21.270 --> 00:46:23.130
at least part of the
results of this paper,

00:46:23.130 --> 00:46:26.890
and then write a report
about your reproducibility.

00:46:26.890 --> 00:46:29.000
And once we have enough
submissions of reports,

00:46:29.000 --> 00:46:30.160
we actually review them,

00:46:30.160 --> 00:46:32.070
peer review the reproducibility reports,

00:46:32.070 --> 00:46:35.140
we select the best one and
annually publish an issue

00:46:35.140 --> 00:46:38.310
in the journal resigns with the
best reproducibility report,

00:46:38.310 --> 00:46:41.280
so you can go and see
the issues of the journal

00:46:41.280 --> 00:46:43.330
from the last two years are out already

00:46:43.330 --> 00:46:46.530
and we're going to be preparing
a new iteration for 2020.

00:46:46.530 --> 00:46:48.780
So this is another place
you can get involved,

00:46:48.780 --> 00:46:50.520
even if you're not at
the stage of research

00:46:50.520 --> 00:46:54.520
to produce your own innovative research,

00:46:54.520 --> 00:46:57.310
you can certainly be a citizen scientist

00:46:57.310 --> 00:47:00.800
and participate by picking up work

00:47:00.800 --> 00:47:03.460
that has been done and reproducing this.

00:47:03.460 --> 00:47:05.520
You know, in many ways and
lots of other sciences,

00:47:05.520 --> 00:47:08.350
we spend a lot of time
learning the techniques

00:47:08.350 --> 00:47:10.770
before carrying our own experiments,

00:47:10.770 --> 00:47:13.230
and in machine learning, I
think this is a great way

00:47:13.230 --> 00:47:17.200
to learn the techniques, to
develop your own expertise

00:47:17.200 --> 00:47:19.850
in running some solid experiments.

00:47:19.850 --> 00:47:22.490
And so if we look at what's
nuisance, maybe 2018,

00:47:22.490 --> 00:47:25.180
when we started talking
about reproducibility

00:47:25.180 --> 00:47:26.530
in machine learning,

00:47:26.530 --> 00:47:28.500
at the time, I sort of gave a keynote talk

00:47:28.500 --> 00:47:30.100
at nerds trying to call,

00:47:30.100 --> 00:47:32.980
to bring to the attention of the community

00:47:32.980 --> 00:47:34.910
that we should be doing
better in that respect.

00:47:34.910 --> 00:47:36.160
Many things have happened,

00:47:36.160 --> 00:47:40.240
we have code submission
policies at most conferences.

00:47:40.240 --> 00:47:44.040
There's now a code tab that's
integrated in archives,

00:47:44.040 --> 00:47:45.420
so when you look at a paper in archive,

00:47:45.420 --> 00:47:47.070
you can also access the code,

00:47:47.070 --> 00:47:49.030
and more recently the data set.

00:47:49.030 --> 00:47:51.460
There's a lot of tooling that allows you

00:47:51.460 --> 00:47:54.600
to sort of create a
tight repo of your work,

00:47:54.600 --> 00:47:57.550
I'm suggesting a few of them
here, but there are more,

00:47:57.550 --> 00:48:00.210
there's the reproducibility
checklist I mentioned,

00:48:00.210 --> 00:48:01.660
the reproducibility challenge,

00:48:01.660 --> 00:48:04.190
that issue of the journal resigns.

00:48:04.190 --> 00:48:06.650
So all elements that have been put in,

00:48:06.650 --> 00:48:09.800
in a sense we didn't invent
and you use science to do this,

00:48:09.800 --> 00:48:13.720
we just made it common in the culture

00:48:13.720 --> 00:48:15.910
and in the practice of our community

00:48:15.910 --> 00:48:18.570
to have higher standards
of reproducibility,

00:48:18.570 --> 00:48:21.650
which we're hoping will
help us as a community,

00:48:21.650 --> 00:48:26.650
have more effect and more
impact with our research.

00:48:26.960 --> 00:48:27.793
I'm going to conclude,

00:48:27.793 --> 00:48:29.240
so I have time for a few more questions

00:48:29.240 --> 00:48:31.210
before we have to close the session.

00:48:31.210 --> 00:48:35.850
I think my main message is
really to keep top of mind

00:48:35.850 --> 00:48:38.420
focused on the fact that science research

00:48:38.420 --> 00:48:41.780
is a collective activity,
collective institution,

00:48:41.780 --> 00:48:44.730
and together we aim to
understand and explain,

00:48:44.730 --> 00:48:46.680
that's really the goal of what we're doing

00:48:46.680 --> 00:48:50.510
and good practices in
terms of reproducibility,

00:48:50.510 --> 00:48:54.153
usability, robustness are
important to contribute to this.

00:48:55.870 --> 00:48:58.730
I will close here a quick acknowledgement

00:48:58.730 --> 00:49:00.430
to the many, many collaborators.

00:49:00.430 --> 00:49:02.390
who participate in the
work I described today,

00:49:02.390 --> 00:49:04.830
who have been really inspiring me

00:49:04.830 --> 00:49:06.740
to keep on doing this work.

00:49:06.740 --> 00:49:09.350
Some of them were participants
in the summer school

00:49:09.350 --> 00:49:12.580
a few years ago, and now
autonomous researchers,

00:49:12.580 --> 00:49:16.803
so thank you to all of them,
and I will take some questions.

00:49:19.310 --> 00:49:21.840
- Fantastic, talk to all really wonderful.

00:49:21.840 --> 00:49:26.840
Okay, so first question
from Martin Varianke.

00:49:26.950 --> 00:49:29.050
Can you share with us a project example,

00:49:29.050 --> 00:49:30.900
which could act as a gold standard

00:49:30.900 --> 00:49:33.030
to what we should consider a reproducible

00:49:33.030 --> 00:49:34.693
or generalizable project?

00:49:40.170 --> 00:49:44.170
I'd have to dig in to come
up with with one of them.

00:49:44.170 --> 00:49:45.780
I think one place to start

00:49:45.780 --> 00:49:49.640
is actually in the journal rescience

00:49:49.640 --> 00:49:51.700
where we've done the
reproducibility issues,

00:49:51.700 --> 00:49:53.280
and I'll be sure to add
that like on the side

00:49:53.280 --> 00:49:55.250
where you can actually access that easily.

00:49:55.250 --> 00:49:57.830
There's a number of
reproducibility reports there,

00:49:57.830 --> 00:50:00.550
and you'll get a really nuanced discussion

00:50:00.550 --> 00:50:02.730
of which were, you know, good or bad,

00:50:02.730 --> 00:50:05.270
these are all based on
recently published papers.

00:50:05.270 --> 00:50:07.340
So it will give you some concrete examples

00:50:07.340 --> 00:50:08.900
that have been vetted, peer reviewed,

00:50:08.900 --> 00:50:11.650
both the original paper and
the reproducibility report.

00:50:13.690 --> 00:50:14.533
- Okay, great.

00:50:16.242 --> 00:50:18.810
Could you please provide some
examples, papers, et cetera,

00:50:18.810 --> 00:50:20.560
of current applications of RL,

00:50:20.560 --> 00:50:22.300
in terms of simulating the environment

00:50:22.300 --> 00:50:24.810
after multiple agents take actions,

00:50:24.810 --> 00:50:27.512
so it's a little bit of a different angle.

00:50:27.512 --> 00:50:28.345
- Simulating the environment

00:50:28.345 --> 00:50:31.513
after multiple agents take actions.

00:50:32.370 --> 00:50:35.250
Yeah, I'd have to think a
little bit about that one,

00:50:35.250 --> 00:50:38.030
maybe someone wants to follow
up with me an email on that,

00:50:38.030 --> 00:50:40.690
or I think we're going to
do some Q&amp;A in writing,

00:50:40.690 --> 00:50:42.560
so maybe move that to the writing section

00:50:42.560 --> 00:50:46.490
and I'll try to dig up some examples.

00:50:46.490 --> 00:50:49.020
I will say I've seen
some interesting cases

00:50:49.020 --> 00:50:52.240
in autonomous driving,
where there's multi agents

00:50:52.240 --> 00:50:55.180
that are all carrying actions
that depend on each other.

00:50:55.180 --> 00:50:58.390
I've also seen some
interesting cases in games,

00:50:58.390 --> 00:50:59.770
we had a paper a few years ago

00:50:59.770 --> 00:51:03.160
on playing the game of
diplomacy with multiple agents,

00:51:03.160 --> 00:51:07.640
so those are a couple, I think,
fun applications of this.

00:51:07.640 --> 00:51:11.030
- Cool, question from Adrienne Kindle,

00:51:11.030 --> 00:51:14.140
a cause of the reproducibility crisis

00:51:14.140 --> 00:51:16.120
is the selection bias in publishing

00:51:16.120 --> 00:51:18.530
where insignificant results
were rarely published,

00:51:18.530 --> 00:51:21.603
which incentivizes researchers
to pick their best results.

00:51:22.607 --> 00:51:24.600
I think the selection bias
in publishing also exist

00:51:24.600 --> 00:51:26.760
in reproducibility venues

00:51:26.760 --> 00:51:29.490
and could impact which papers are tested.

00:51:29.490 --> 00:51:33.010
What have you found successful
in motivating researchers

00:51:33.010 --> 00:51:34.320
to reproduce research,

00:51:34.320 --> 00:51:37.383
given the risk that results
may not be reproducible?

00:51:38.550 --> 00:51:42.140
- Yeah, that is definitely
a strong factor.

00:51:42.140 --> 00:51:45.410
I think on reproducibility results

00:51:45.410 --> 00:51:47.260
were a little bit more neutral

00:51:47.260 --> 00:51:50.120
because we don't have as
much of a strong party

00:51:50.120 --> 00:51:52.290
of whether it's going to
be reproducible or not,

00:51:52.290 --> 00:51:55.010
we're really engaging
fundamentally with the question.

00:51:55.010 --> 00:51:58.280
So I think it's a little
bit less of an issue.

00:51:58.280 --> 00:52:00.210
There's definitely a
bias, we see, for example,

00:52:00.210 --> 00:52:01.420
in the reproducibility challenge,

00:52:01.420 --> 00:52:03.610
we let people pick what
paper they want to reproduce,

00:52:03.610 --> 00:52:05.500
there's definitely a selection bias there

00:52:05.500 --> 00:52:09.080
of what papers people
are picking versus not.

00:52:09.080 --> 00:52:12.290
And we can nearly cover
the set of them all,

00:52:12.290 --> 00:52:13.710
I think, you know, a
conference like in Europe

00:52:13.710 --> 00:52:15.330
says upwards of 1000 papers

00:52:15.330 --> 00:52:17.380
and through the reproducibility challenge,

00:52:17.380 --> 00:52:20.080
we tackle maybe a couple
of hundred of them.

00:52:20.080 --> 00:52:21.833
So we're not close to coverage.

00:52:22.720 --> 00:52:23.760
One of the things we've done

00:52:23.760 --> 00:52:26.410
is really encouraged this challenge

00:52:26.410 --> 00:52:28.610
to be taken on as a project

00:52:28.610 --> 00:52:30.810
in machine learning graduate courses,

00:52:30.810 --> 00:52:33.490
I think it makes a great final
project for anyone out there.

00:52:33.490 --> 00:52:36.710
If all, you know, graduate
level machine learning courses

00:52:36.710 --> 00:52:40.090
would take this on as
their final course project,

00:52:40.090 --> 00:52:42.810
would really make a dent in terms of that,

00:52:42.810 --> 00:52:44.840
and really reduce that selection bias,

00:52:44.840 --> 00:52:46.707
which is problematic right now.

00:52:49.070 --> 00:52:53.140
- Great, how do you think
we can encourage researchers

00:52:53.140 --> 00:52:55.650
or organizations to
enforce better standards

00:52:55.650 --> 00:52:57.020
in a systematic way,

00:52:57.020 --> 00:53:00.080
ie not hope the authors
are well-intentioned,

00:53:00.080 --> 00:53:03.540
can we enforce it more in hindsight?

00:53:03.540 --> 00:53:06.760
- Yeah, for the last three years now,

00:53:06.760 --> 00:53:10.070
Neuro Rx has adopted the
aversion of the checklist.

00:53:10.070 --> 00:53:12.070
and it's evolved a little
bit through the years,

00:53:12.070 --> 00:53:15.040
but the checklist is used
at these major conferences

00:53:15.040 --> 00:53:18.360
like Neuro, CML as part
of the submission process,

00:53:18.360 --> 00:53:20.110
so the authors actually
have to fill it out

00:53:20.110 --> 00:53:22.850
and then it's actually
visible to the reviewers,

00:53:22.850 --> 00:53:26.410
and so there's an
accountability mechanism there

00:53:26.410 --> 00:53:28.350
that gets built in this year.

00:53:28.350 --> 00:53:29.740
It was particularly clever,

00:53:29.740 --> 00:53:33.310
they built it into the
lead tech style file

00:53:33.310 --> 00:53:36.560
that the authors have to use,
so it's really, you know,

00:53:36.560 --> 00:53:37.930
changing that behavior

00:53:37.930 --> 00:53:41.880
through the structure and
the tooling that's provided,

00:53:41.880 --> 00:53:43.680
I think that's one of
the most effective things

00:53:43.680 --> 00:53:46.393
we can do to change this.

00:53:48.970 --> 00:53:50.480
- I like this question,

00:53:50.480 --> 00:53:52.850
so very often reviewers suspect

00:53:52.850 --> 00:53:54.800
of cherry picking up RL environments

00:53:54.800 --> 00:53:56.390
because only if you're presented,

00:53:56.390 --> 00:53:59.570
while for small research
centers with limited resources,

00:53:59.570 --> 00:54:01.820
those are the only ones
that could be tested,

00:54:01.820 --> 00:54:03.770
while big brands have plenty of machines

00:54:03.770 --> 00:54:05.760
and can show lots of environments,

00:54:05.760 --> 00:54:08.350
how can the community be improved

00:54:08.350 --> 00:54:10.070
with common criteria for everyone,

00:54:10.070 --> 00:54:13.860
and I think balancing the, like, you know,

00:54:13.860 --> 00:54:15.700
the availability of compute

00:54:15.700 --> 00:54:18.900
with the requirements reproducibility?

00:54:18.900 --> 00:54:22.190
- Yeah, it's always a challenge,

00:54:22.190 --> 00:54:23.840
like if you're picking the environment,

00:54:23.840 --> 00:54:25.720
you're essentially like setting the bar,

00:54:25.720 --> 00:54:27.370
and then you're showing that you can,

00:54:27.370 --> 00:54:28.750
you know, jump over the bar.

00:54:28.750 --> 00:54:31.370
That's always a bit of
a conflict of interest,

00:54:31.370 --> 00:54:34.010
so, and then at the same
time, we have to acknowledge

00:54:34.010 --> 00:54:36.550
that the resources are
not always the same.

00:54:36.550 --> 00:54:41.420
I want to point out a paper
that was written by Pablo Castro

00:54:41.420 --> 00:54:43.960
and some of his colleagues
at Google last year,

00:54:43.960 --> 00:54:45.600
I think it was really showing

00:54:45.600 --> 00:54:48.410
that there's still a lot of
value that can be obtained

00:54:48.410 --> 00:54:51.100
from looking at smaller domains
and reinforcement learning.

00:54:51.100 --> 00:54:55.080
It's a beautiful paper that
I think is worth considering,

00:54:55.080 --> 00:54:57.560
especially for people
who come particularly

00:54:57.560 --> 00:55:02.560
from institutions where you
have small access to data,

00:55:02.770 --> 00:55:06.270
perhaps not data, but compute.

00:55:06.270 --> 00:55:08.110
The way I always think about it

00:55:08.110 --> 00:55:11.210
when I discuss with my students
and it happens to us all,

00:55:11.210 --> 00:55:13.550
so it's always like, yes,

00:55:13.550 --> 00:55:15.990
it's fine to show results
on a small set of domains,

00:55:15.990 --> 00:55:18.140
but are you the one who
are picking the domain

00:55:18.140 --> 00:55:20.520
or can we actually lean
on picking the domains

00:55:20.520 --> 00:55:21.900
that someone else has picked?

00:55:21.900 --> 00:55:23.550
So we pick these five domains

00:55:23.550 --> 00:55:25.350
because these were the five domains

00:55:25.350 --> 00:55:26.750
that were used using this other paper?

00:55:26.750 --> 00:55:31.200
So we're not picking them
ourselves, we're adopting them

00:55:31.200 --> 00:55:32.690
because someone else suggested them.

00:55:32.690 --> 00:55:36.270
So I think there you
reduce the cherry picking.

00:55:36.270 --> 00:55:40.450
The other thing is to some
degree, selecting environments

00:55:40.450 --> 00:55:42.520
is perfectly acceptable

00:55:42.520 --> 00:55:45.570
if you explain what's
your selection criteria.

00:55:45.570 --> 00:55:49.450
And so I think we're at a phase
of maturity of our research,

00:55:49.450 --> 00:55:51.530
that it's very rare that
you will publish an app

00:55:51.530 --> 00:55:53.130
or find, discover an algorithm

00:55:53.130 --> 00:55:55.730
that will be better on every domain,

00:55:55.730 --> 00:55:57.610
everywhere all the time, right?

00:55:57.610 --> 00:56:01.430
So if you can focus on a
smaller class of domains,

00:56:01.430 --> 00:56:04.840
but you explain why for
that class of domain,

00:56:04.840 --> 00:56:07.190
this type of algorithm is better,

00:56:07.190 --> 00:56:09.827
then I think that's
perfectly reasonable to do,

00:56:09.827 --> 00:56:12.063
but you know, the cherry picking is not.

00:56:15.370 --> 00:56:17.480
- Great, I also like this question,

00:56:17.480 --> 00:56:21.440
so it's hardest not to fool ourselves.

00:56:21.440 --> 00:56:23.210
Well, what questions can we ask,

00:56:23.210 --> 00:56:26.130
what tests can we run
to prevent human nature,

00:56:26.130 --> 00:56:28.570
especially for someone newer to research?

00:56:28.570 --> 00:56:30.080
It's very hard, right?

00:56:30.080 --> 00:56:32.360
- Richard Fineman has a
beautiful quote of that,

00:56:32.360 --> 00:56:34.650
which I sometimes use in
some of my talks about,

00:56:34.650 --> 00:56:36.640
you know, you don't want to
fool anyone, but you know,

00:56:36.640 --> 00:56:38.350
the easiest person to fool is yourself,

00:56:38.350 --> 00:56:40.270
something to that effect.

00:56:40.270 --> 00:56:42.960
Yeah, to me, that goes
back to the mindset.

00:56:42.960 --> 00:56:46.290
It goes back to a mindset of
inquiry and an understanding,

00:56:46.290 --> 00:56:50.140
and it goes back to a strong
commitment to transparency,

00:56:50.140 --> 00:56:51.440
and really finding

00:56:51.440 --> 00:56:56.440
that that is what drives
us as researchers,

00:56:56.550 --> 00:56:59.030
otherwise we're sort
of in the wrong field.

00:56:59.030 --> 00:57:01.170
One thing I didn't say is
all the work that we did

00:57:01.170 --> 00:57:03.360
on reproducibility that I shared today,

00:57:03.360 --> 00:57:05.220
you know, the comparison of the results,

00:57:05.220 --> 00:57:07.000
the not doing the top three picks

00:57:07.000 --> 00:57:09.420
and so on all of that
came about in our lab,

00:57:09.420 --> 00:57:12.410
because not because we
set out to do this at all,

00:57:12.410 --> 00:57:14.690
we were trying to produce
the standard paper

00:57:14.690 --> 00:57:15.730
where we had a new method

00:57:15.730 --> 00:57:17.980
that was better than
all these other methods,

00:57:18.910 --> 00:57:20.490
and yet it wasn't,

00:57:20.490 --> 00:57:23.440
and as we started digging
into why it wasn't,

00:57:23.440 --> 00:57:24.850
this is where we discovered

00:57:24.850 --> 00:57:28.290
all these really odd things
that other people were doing,

00:57:28.290 --> 00:57:31.410
and so at that point, we sort of pivoted

00:57:31.410 --> 00:57:34.460
and decided like this was
the more interesting story,

00:57:34.460 --> 00:57:36.790
it was no longer a story
about showing our algorithm

00:57:36.790 --> 00:57:39.490
it was more interesting,
it was much more the story

00:57:39.490 --> 00:57:42.380
about what were some of the practices.

00:57:42.380 --> 00:57:44.300
So sometimes, you know,
keeping an open mind

00:57:44.300 --> 00:57:48.230
about what is the interesting
part of the research story

00:57:48.230 --> 00:57:52.696
that you are apt to tell
at a particular time point

00:57:52.696 --> 00:57:56.840
in our field is it's something
that you should follow.

00:57:56.840 --> 00:57:59.360
It's harder publishing a
paper with negative results,

00:57:59.360 --> 00:58:01.920
it's awesome, but it's harder.

00:58:01.920 --> 00:58:04.810
So, you know, really
solicit all the advice

00:58:04.810 --> 00:58:07.450
and the support you can get
from experts and colleagues,

00:58:07.450 --> 00:58:10.760
lean into that collective
wisdom to do that kind of work.

00:58:10.760 --> 00:58:14.373
It's really rewarding when you do it well.

00:58:15.440 --> 00:58:18.220
- Yeah, that's the great
example of following your,

00:58:18.220 --> 00:58:20.800
I guess, scientific and
intellectual curiosity,

00:58:20.800 --> 00:58:23.250
and I'll look at how much
impact this work has had,

00:58:23.250 --> 00:58:25.380
which probably, I don't
know how great the method

00:58:25.380 --> 00:58:27.480
that you're thinking of publishing was,

00:58:27.480 --> 00:58:31.150
but certainly the impact
of this work has been huge.

00:58:31.150 --> 00:58:32.530
I think we should probably close it there.

00:58:32.530 --> 00:58:35.430
We're two minutes over,
thank you so much, Joelle

00:58:35.430 --> 00:58:37.830
for the excellent talk,
very thought provoking

00:58:37.830 --> 00:58:40.310
and wonderful as always.

00:58:40.310 --> 00:58:44.120
And so with that, I'll close
this procession of the day

00:58:44.120 --> 00:58:46.320
and we'll look forward to
seeing everybody later on

00:58:46.320 --> 00:58:47.810
in the further lectures

00:58:47.810 --> 00:58:50.210
and then the career fair
and everything else.

00:58:50.210 --> 00:58:51.043
Thanks again.

00:58:51.043 --> 00:58:53.460
- Thank you, thanks Liam,
thanks everyone, bye bye.

00:58:54.437 --> 00:58:57.437
(soft upbeat music)

